      "problem": "$\\,$ $a$ is odd and $b$ is odd.\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "De Morgan's law for conjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$a$ is odd and $b$ is odd\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(a\\text{ is odd and }b\\text{ is odd}) & ≡¬(a\\text{ is odd}∧b\\text{ is odd}) \\\\  & ≡¬(a\\text{ is odd})∨¬(b\\text{ is odd}) \\\\  & ≡a\\text{ is even}∨b\\text{ is even} \\\\  & ≡a\\text{ is even or }b\\text{ is even}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
